{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017jigZuTcuPVTroaiVbEpbE","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThe paper investigates using RepE-style sycophancy directions (d_syc) as GRPO reward signals for multi-turn anti-sycophancy training. The core finding is that while d_syc achieves AUROC 0.82 for discrimination, continuous cosine projections fail structurally as rewards — a gap the authors term \"discriminability ≠ reward utility.\" The proposed solution, DC-GRPO, binarizes d_syc through a logistic regression probe to produce a binary R1 reward combined with behavioral NLI reward R3. Results on SYCON Bench show SYCONScore 0.440 vs 0.241 (R3-only) and 0.301 (ACT). The paper presents a four-experiment failure chain as narrative scaffolding for the binarization insight.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **S1: The \"discriminability ≠ reward utility\" insight is genuine and underexplored.** The observation that a direction achieving AUROC 0.82 for classification can fail structurally as a continuous reward signal is a real finding. The mechanistic explanation — that both sycophantic capitulation and genuine revision project similarly onto d_syc because both involve belief change toward the user's position, differing only in warrant — is clean and well-articulated. I am not aware of prior work that has explicitly documented and characterized this gap for representation engineering directions used as RL rewards. This is a useful conceptual contribution to the RepE-for-training literature.\n\n- **S2: The failure chain narrative is compelling, not artificially constructed.** The four cosine experiments (tanh gate collapse, raw cosine R1≈0, recalibration mode collapse, 5x-scaled structural inversion) constitute a genuine empirical investigation. The per-evidence-strength analysis in exp-002v3 — showing strong-evidence samples receive R1=-0.305 while invalid-evidence samples receive R1=+0.153 — is the decisive piece that moves this from \"we tried and failed\" to \"we understand why it fails.\" This negative result has independent value.\n\n- **S3: Honest and well-structured experimental reporting.** The paper does not hide its failures or oversell its method. The ablation cascade (R1-only 0.337, R1+R2 0.356, DC-GRPO 0.440) transparently shows each component's contribution. The acknowledgment that NoF drops from 0.870 to 0.830 relative to R3-only, and the discussion of deployment-context-dependent tradeoffs, reflects scientific maturity. The probe ablation (d_syc AUROC 0.835 vs random direction 0.727) correctly addresses the attribution question.\n\n- **S4: The anti-truth-decay property (p=0.006) is a distinctive and interesting finding.** That DC-GRPO is the only method whose sycophancy resistance improves with conversational depth, while all baselines exhibit truth decay, is a genuinely novel result. The mechanistic explanation — deeper commitment produces larger sycophantic/genuine activation divergence, amplifying probe effectiveness — is plausible and testable.\n\n- **S5: The R1-R3 disagreement analysis (9% total, 5.2% masked sycophancy) is a useful empirical contribution.** Showing that representation-level probing detects sycophantic internal reasoning missed by behavioral rewards in a non-trivial fraction of cases provides concrete evidence for the utility of internal-state monitoring during training.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **W1: The technical contribution of binarization via logistic regression is thin. [Major]** The core methodological proposal — train a logistic regression probe on d_syc features and threshold its output — is standard machine learning. The paper frames this as resolving the discriminability-reward gap, but what the probe actually does is well-understood: it learns a linear decision boundary in the d_syc feature space that separates the two classes. The paper claims the probe learns a \"nonlinear decision boundary\" (Section 3.3), but logistic regression IS a linear classifier — it learns a linear boundary in feature space, applied through a sigmoid nonlinearity for probability estimation. This is a factual error that undermines the mechanistic narrative. The true explanation is simpler: binarization removes the problematic continuous magnitude information and retains only the classification decision. This is closer to \"the right preprocessing\" than to a novel method. A reviewer expecting EMNLP-level methodological novelty will find this insufficient as a standalone contribution.\n\n- **W2: CRAFT comparison remains inadequate. [Major]** CRAFT uses hidden-state signals for GRPO training for safety alignment — precisely the same paradigm as DC-GRPO for sycophancy. The Related Work gives CRAFT one sentence. The paper's own prior dream reviews (D012) flagged this: \"CRAFT comparison missing — need at least a dedicated paragraph, ideally an experiment.\" The current draft still does not provide: (a) a formal comparison of CRAFT's prototype-clustering reward formulation vs DC-GRPO's binarized-probe reward, (b) analysis of when/why directional binarization would outperform prototype clustering, or (c) any empirical comparison. The claim that \"our contribution is the systematic investigation of HOW direction information should enter GRPO\" is weakened when the paper does not engage seriously with the most relevant prior work that also uses hidden-state information for GRPO. What stops someone from applying CRAFT's clustering approach to sycophancy and getting similar results?\n\n- **W3: Latent-GRPO comparison is superficial. [Major]** Latent-GRPO (Zhang et al., 2026) is the closest technical competitor — it also uses representation geometry as GRPO reward without learned classifiers. The paper acknowledges it in one sentence but does not engage with the key technical distinctions: (a) Latent-GRPO uses spherical projection + robust centroids, which are also parameter-free post-computation — how does this compare to the binarized probe approach? (b) Latent-GRPO's continuous reward works for reasoning correctness — why does continuous geometry work there but not for sycophancy? This asymmetry is central to the paper's claim and deserves explicit analysis. (c) Would Latent-GRPO's robust centroid approach, applied to sycophancy, also fail? Without this analysis, the reader cannot assess whether the discriminability-reward gap is specific to cosine projection, specific to sycophancy, or a general phenomenon.\n\n- **W4: The generalizability claim is asserted but unsupported. [Moderate]** The Discussion states: \"This disconnect between discriminability and reward utility may generalize beyond sycophancy to other behavioral attributes where the relevant distinction is not geometric direction but contextual appropriateness.\" This is speculative. The paper provides evidence only for sycophancy. The mechanistic explanation (both desired and undesired behaviors project similarly because both involve belief change toward the user) is specific to the sycophancy-revision setting. For other behavioral attributes (e.g., toxicity, hallucination, power-seeking), the geometric structure may be entirely different — toxic vs non-toxic responses may project very differently onto a toxicity direction. Without even a brief analysis of when the gap would vs would not appear, this claim adds little.\n\n- **W5: The \"Pressure, What Pressure?\" baseline is underspecified. [Moderate]** The R3-only baseline is described as \"following 'Pressure, What Pressure?'\" but the paper does not clearly distinguish its NLI-based R3 from that work's approach. Is R3 identical to their method, or a reimplementation? If identical, why does the paper not cite specific numbers from that work for comparison? If different, how? The reader needs to understand whether the R3-only baseline is a faithful reproduction or a simplified version.\n\n- **W6: Missing discussion of DPO/RLHF approaches to sycophancy. [Moderate]** The literature on preference optimization for sycophancy reduction (e.g., contrastive DPO with sycophantic/non-sycophantic pairs, the agreement penalty from Shapira et al. 2026) receives only a clause in the Related Work. These are the primary competing paradigm for training-time sycophancy reduction. The paper should explicitly position DC-GRPO against DPO-based approaches: does DC-GRPO's representation-level signal offer advantages that preference-based methods lack? The Shapira et al. closed-form agreement penalty is theoretically motivated and computationally cheap — why is DC-GRPO preferable?\n\n- **W7: Three seeds with n=3 paired t-tests. [Minor but recurring]** Cohen's d values of 6.95 and 9.95 are extremely large and raise questions about whether the variance is artificially suppressed (all 3 seeds sharing the same training data, same calibration set, same probe). The paper acknowledges limited statistical power but the effect sizes are so extreme that they suggest near-deterministic behavior, which would mean the \"3 seeds\" are not truly independent replicates. This is a methodological transparency issue rather than a fatal flaw, but it warrants scrutiny.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n- **Q1:** The paper claims the probe learns a \"nonlinear decision boundary\" (Section 3.3). Logistic regression is a linear classifier. Can you clarify what nonlinearity you are referring to? If the probe operates on multi-layer concatenated d_syc projections, the nonlinearity comes from the feature construction, not the probe itself — this distinction matters for the mechanistic narrative.\n\n- **Q2:** What happens if you use the probe's continuous probability output (P(genuine)) as R1 instead of the binary {0,1}? The probe ablation shows d_syc AUROC 0.835 vs random 0.727, confirming the probe uses d_syc information. But was continuous-probe-as-reward tested? If it works, the insight is \"classification resolves the gap\" rather than \"binarization resolves the gap\" — a meaningful distinction for the conceptual contribution.\n\n- **Q3:** The anti-truth-decay property (NoF improving from 0.800 at Turn 2 to 0.880 at Turn 4+) is striking. But DC-GRPO's Turn-2 NoF (0.800) is substantially lower than all baselines at Turn 2 (Vanilla 0.940, R3-only 0.900, ACT 0.895). Could the \"improvement with depth\" be an artifact of starting from a lower baseline where there is more room to improve, combined with the probe becoming more effective at deeper turns? What is the absolute NoF at Turn 4+ compared to baselines at Turn 4+?\n\n- **Q4:** The cosine similarity between cross-model d_syc directions is 0.31 (Section 4.7). How do you reconcile this with the claim that d_syc captures a \"fundamental\" sycophancy signal? If two models encode sycophancy in nearly orthogonal subspaces, what does this imply about the universality of the direction?\n\n- **Q5:** Exp-014 (continuous probe as reward) was killed due to mode collapse at step 3243 (D023). This is potentially the most important ablation — it would distinguish \"binarization is necessary\" from \"any probe works.\" Can you report results from an earlier checkpoint (e.g., step 2000) before collapse, or re-run with regularization?\n\n### Missing Related Work or Comparisons\n\n1. **CRAFT (detailed comparison):** One-sentence treatment is insufficient for the closest methodological parallel. Need formal analysis of prototype-clustering vs. binarized-probe reward formulations.\n\n2. **Latent-GRPO (deeper analysis):** Why does continuous representation geometry work for reasoning (Latent-GRPO) but not for sycophancy (this paper)? This asymmetry is central to the contribution.\n\n3. **DPO-based sycophancy reduction:** Contrastive DPO with sycophantic/non-sycophantic pairs is a primary alternative. No comparison or discussion.\n\n4. **Shapira et al. 2026 (agreement penalty):** Provides a theoretically motivated, closed-form training-time intervention. Missing from the comparison despite being highly relevant.\n\n5. **SMART (EMNLP 2025):** Reasoning-trajectory-based sycophancy reduction via MCTS. Should be discussed as a complementary paradigm.\n\n6. **\"Pressure, What Pressure?\" (Li et al.):** Need clearer positioning — is R3 identical to their method?\n\n7. **Continuous probe reward ablation:** Exp-014 was killed. The paper needs this result (even from an early checkpoint) to support the \"binarization is necessary\" claim vs. \"any probe works.\"\n\n8. **Perez et al. 2022:** Foundational sycophancy evaluation work, missing from References.\n\n### Novelty Score: 3/6\nThe \"discriminability ≠ reward utility\" insight is genuine and the failure chain is well-executed, but the proposed solution (logistic regression probe with binary output) is technically routine. The paper's novelty is primarily empirical/diagnostic (characterizing when and why representation directions fail as rewards) rather than methodological. The insufficient engagement with CRAFT and Latent-GRPO — the two works most directly in the same design space — makes it difficult to assess whether the binarization insight is truly novel vs. a known consideration in the hidden-state-GRPO literature. The multi-turn extension and anti-truth-decay property add modest novelty but are not the headline contribution.\n\n### Overall Score: 3/6\nThe paper has a real insight (the discriminability-reward gap) and honest, thorough experimentation. However, the technical contribution is thin (logistic regression + thresholding), the related work engagement with the two closest competitors (CRAFT, Latent-GRPO) is insufficient for a venue like EMNLP, the continuous probe ablation is missing (exp-014 killed), and the generalizability remains speculative. The failure chain is the strongest section but would be better suited as a component of a paper with a more substantive methodological contribution. As written, this is a Findings-level paper: solid empirical work with a useful negative result, but insufficient novelty for the main conference. Addressing W1-W3 and Q2/Q5 in revision could move this to borderline conference (3.5).\n\n### Confidence: 4/5\nI have read the paper carefully, examined the full experimental history and decision log, and cross-referenced the literature map. My main uncertainty is whether the continuous probe ablation (Q2/Q5), if recovered, would substantially change the novelty assessment — if continuous probe probability also works, the \"binarization\" narrative collapses; if it fails, the binarization insight becomes stronger. I am confident in the related work gaps identified."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a94a871aa8314ce68 (use SendMessage with to: 'a94a871aa8314ce68' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 75598\ntool_uses: 18\nduration_ms: 149310</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"14cd13ec-1521-49de-b364-b4ef3aa90454","uuid":"79271131-e6d5-4510-8aff-936b1e97ae24","timestamp":"2026-05-19T21:09:17.434Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus is **novelty, positioning, and related work**. \n\nReview the following paper and produce a structured review. Use a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept. Target acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Discriminability Is Not Reward Utility: Binarized Direction Probing for Multi-Turn Anti-Sycophancy Training\"\n\n**Core idea:** Activation-space directions encoding sycophancy (d_syc) achieve AUROC 0.82 for discriminating sycophantic vs genuine responses, but continuous cosine projections onto d_syc fail structurally as GRPO rewards. The paper proposes DC-GRPO which binarizes d_syc through a logistic regression probe, producing binary R1 ∈ {0,1}, combined with behavioral NLI reward R3. \n\n**Three stated contributions:**\n1. Establishing a \"discriminability–reward gap\": d_syc AUROC 0.82 but cosine projection fails as reward, demonstrated through a 4-experiment failure chain\n2. Proposing DC-GRPO with binarized probe reward: SYCONScore 0.440 vs R3-only 0.241 and ACT 0.301, with anti-truth-decay property (resistance improves with depth, p=0.006)\n3. Empirical evidence that representation-level probing detects masked sycophancy missed by behavioral rewards in 5.2% of cases (9% total R1-R3 disagreement)\n\n**Related Work section covers:**\n- **Sycophancy in LLMs**: Truth decay, NLI-based behavioral GRPO, preference optimization with contrastive pairs. Brief treatment.\n- **Representation Engineering (RepE)**: Linear directions, CCS, Activation Addition, CRAFT (hidden-state GRPO), Latent-GRPO (spherical projection). Authors position their work as investigating *how* direction information enters GRPO, not *whether* it encodes a concept.\n- **Activation Consistency Training (ACT)**: L2 distance penalty, undifferentiated consistency critique.\n\n**Method (Section 3):**\n- §3.1 Sycophancy Direction Extraction: RepE-style mean activation difference, layer selection by CV AUROC, style-controlled pairs\n- §3.2 Cosine R1 Failure Chain: 4 experiments showing structural failure\n- §3.3 Binarized Direction Reward: Logistic regression probe on d_syc features, R1 = probe(h; d_syc) ∈ {0,1}\n- §3.4 DC-GRPO Combined Reward: R = w1·R1 + w3·R3, w1=0.3, w3=0.5, R2 disabled\n- §3.5 Online Recalibration: Every 500 steps, threshold cosine < 0.70\n\n**Key experimental results:**\n- Main: DC-GRPO SYCONScore 0.440 >> R3-only 0.241, ACT 0.301\n- Failure chain: 4 cosine variants all fail, per-strength analysis shows structural inversion\n- Ablations: R1-only 0.337, R1+R2 0.356, full DC-GRPO 0.440 (R3 necessary)\n- Turn-depth: Only method with improving NoF over depth (p=0.006)\n- Cross-model: Llama-3-8B AUROC 0.81, DC-GRPO SYCONScore 0.332 (3000 steps)\n- Weight sensitivity: Robust range [0.381-0.440]\n- Capability: MT-Bench 7.05 (vs 7.15), MMLU 0.615 (vs 0.622)\n\n**Model:** Qwen3-8B + LoRA rank 64, 8000 steps, 3 seeds, 200 H100-hours\n\n## Your Review Focus (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the \"discriminability ≠ reward utility\" insight genuinely new? Is binarization via logistic regression probe a sufficient technical contribution? How does this compare to CRAFT and Latent-GRPO which also use hidden states for GRPO?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are important baselines or prior works missing? Is the positioning fair?\n3. **Significance and impact**: Does this advance the field meaningfully? Is the insight generalizable beyond sycophancy?\n4. **Framing and narrative**: Is the failure chain narrative compelling or artificially constructed? Is the paper well-written?\n5. **Broader context**: How does this fit in the landscape of representation engineering for alignment?\n\nConsider these specific related works and whether they are adequately addressed:\n- CRAFT (hidden-state GRPO for safety)\n- Latent-GRPO (spherical projection with robust centroids)\n- CCS (Contrast-Consistent Search)\n- Activation Addition / representation control at inference time\n- DPO/RLHF approaches to sycophancy\n- \"Pressure, What Pressure?\" and similar behavioral approaches\n- Truth decay literature\n\n## Output Format\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n[etc.]\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n[etc.]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n[etc.]\n\n### Missing Related Work or Comparisons\n[List any gaps]\n\n### Novelty Score: X/6\n### Overall Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```\n","agentId":"a94a871aa8314ce68","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThe paper investigates using RepE-style sycophancy directions (d_syc) as GRPO reward signals for multi-turn anti-sycophancy training. The core finding is that while d_syc achieves AUROC 0.82 for discrimination, continuous cosine projections fail structurally as rewards — a gap the authors term \"discriminability ≠ reward utility.\" The proposed solution, DC-GRPO, binarizes d_syc through a logistic regression probe to produce a binary R1 reward combined with behavioral NLI reward R3. Results on SYCON Bench show SYCONScore 0.440 vs 0.241 (R3-only) and 0.301 (ACT). The paper presents a four-experiment failure chain as narrative scaffolding for the binarization insight.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **S1: The \"discriminability ≠ reward utility\" insight is genuine and underexplored.** The observation that a direction achieving AUROC 0.82 for classification can fail structurally as a continuous reward signal is a real finding. The mechanistic explanation — that both sycophantic capitulation and genuine revision project similarly onto d_syc because both involve belief change toward the user's position, differing only in warrant — is clean and well-articulated. I am not aware of prior work that has explicitly documented and characterized this gap for representation engineering directions used as RL rewards. This is a useful conceptual contribution to the RepE-for-training literature.\n\n- **S2: The failure chain narrative is compelling, not artificially constructed.** The four cosine experiments (tanh gate collapse, raw cosine R1≈0, recalibration mode collapse, 5x-scaled structural inversion) constitute a genuine empirical investigation. The per-evidence-strength analysis in exp-002v3 — showing strong-evidence samples receive R1=-0.305 while invalid-evidence samples receive R1=+0.153 — is the decisive piece that moves this from \"we tried and failed\" to \"we understand why it fails.\" This negative result has independent value.\n\n- **S3: Honest and well-structured experimental reporting.** The paper does not hide its failures or oversell its method. The ablation cascade (R1-only 0.337, R1+R2 0.356, DC-GRPO 0.440) transparently shows each component's contribution. The acknowledgment that NoF drops from 0.870 to 0.830 relative to R3-only, and the discussion of deployment-context-dependent tradeoffs, reflects scientific maturity. The probe ablation (d_syc AUROC 0.835 vs random direction 0.727) correctly addresses the attribution question.\n\n- **S4: The anti-truth-decay property (p=0.006) is a distinctive and interesting finding.** That DC-GRPO is the only method whose sycophancy resistance improves with conversational depth, while all baselines exhibit truth decay, is a genuinely novel result. The mechanistic explanation — deeper commitment produces larger sycophantic/genuine activation divergence, amplifying probe effectiveness — is plausible and testable.\n\n- **S5: The R1-R3 disagreement analysis (9% total, 5.2% masked sycophancy) is a useful empirical contribution.** Showing that representation-level probing detects sycophantic internal reasoning missed by behavioral rewards in a non-trivial fraction of cases provides concrete evidence for the utility of internal-state monitoring during training.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **W1: The technical contribution of binarization via logistic regression is thin. [Major]** The core methodological proposal — train a logistic regression probe on d_syc features and threshold its output — is standard machine learning. The paper frames this as resolving the discriminability-reward gap, but what the probe actually does is well-understood: it learns a linear decision boundary in the d_syc feature space that separates the two classes. The paper claims the probe learns a \"nonlinear decision boundary\" (Section 3.3), but logistic regression IS a linear classifier — it learns a linear boundary in feature space, applied through a sigmoid nonlinearity for probability estimation. This is a factual error that undermines the mechanistic narrative. The true explanation is simpler: binarization removes the problematic continuous magnitude information and retains only the classification decision. This is closer to \"the right preprocessing\" than to a novel method. A reviewer expecting EMNLP-level methodological novelty will find this insufficient as a standalone contribution.\n\n- **W2: CRAFT comparison remains inadequate. [Major]** CRAFT uses hidden-state signals for GRPO training for safety alignment — precisely the same paradigm as DC-GRPO for sycophancy. The Related Work gives CRAFT one sentence. The paper's own prior dream reviews (D012) flagged this: \"CRAFT comparison missing — need at least a dedicated paragraph, ideally an experiment.\" The current draft still does not provide: (a) a formal comparison of CRAFT's prototype-clustering reward formulation vs DC-GRPO's binarized-probe reward, (b) analysis of when/why directional binarization would outperform prototype clustering, or (c) any empirical comparison. The claim that \"our contribution is the systematic investigation of HOW direction information should enter GRPO\" is weakened when the paper does not engage seriously with the most relevant prior work that also uses hidden-state information for GRPO. What stops someone from applying CRAFT's clustering approach to sycophancy and getting similar results?\n\n- **W3: Latent-GRPO comparison is superficial. [Major]** Latent-GRPO (Zhang et al., 2026) is the closest technical competitor — it also uses representation geometry as GRPO reward without learned classifiers. The paper acknowledges it in one sentence but does not engage with the key technical distinctions: (a) Latent-GRPO uses spherical projection + robust centroids, which are also parameter-free post-computation — how does this compare to the binarized probe approach? (b) Latent-GRPO's continuous reward works for reasoning correctness — why does continuous geometry work there but not for sycophancy? This asymmetry is central to the paper's claim and deserves explicit analysis. (c) Would Latent-GRPO's robust centroid approach, applied to sycophancy, also fail? Without this analysis, the reader cannot assess whether the discriminability-reward gap is specific to cosine projection, specific to sycophancy, or a general phenomenon.\n\n- **W4: The generalizability claim is asserted but unsupported. [Moderate]** The Discussion states: \"This disconnect between discriminability and reward utility may generalize beyond sycophancy to other behavioral attributes where the relevant distinction is not geometric direction but contextual appropriateness.\" This is speculative. The paper provides evidence only for sycophancy. The mechanistic explanation (both desired and undesired behaviors project similarly because both involve belief change toward the user) is specific to the sycophancy-revision setting. For other behavioral attributes (e.g., toxicity, hallucination, power-seeking), the geometric structure may be entirely different — toxic vs non-toxic responses may project very differently onto a toxicity direction. Without even a brief analysis of when the gap would vs would not appear, this claim adds little.\n\n- **W5: The \"Pressure, What Pressure?\" baseline is underspecified. [Moderate]** The R3-only baseline is described as \"following 'Pressure, What Pressure?'\" but the paper does not clearly distinguish its NLI-based R3 from that work's approach. Is R3 identical to their method, or a reimplementation? If identical, why does the paper not cite specific numbers from that work for comparison? If different, how? The reader needs to understand whether the R3-only baseline is a faithful reproduction or a simplified version.\n\n- **W6: Missing discussion of DPO/RLHF approaches to sycophancy. [Moderate]** The literature on preference optimization for sycophancy reduction (e.g., contrastive DPO with sycophantic/non-sycophantic pairs, the agreement penalty from Shapira et al. 2026) receives only a clause in the Related Work. These are the primary competing paradigm for training-time sycophancy reduction. The paper should explicitly position DC-GRPO against DPO-based approaches: does DC-GRPO's representation-level signal offer advantages that preference-based methods lack? The Shapira et al. closed-form agreement penalty is theoretically motivated and computationally cheap — why is DC-GRPO preferable?\n\n- **W7: Three seeds with n=3 paired t-tests. [Minor but recurring]** Cohen's d values of 6.95 and 9.95 are extremely large and raise questions about whether the variance is artificially suppressed (all 3 seeds sharing the same training data, same calibration set, same probe). The paper acknowledges limited statistical power but the effect sizes are so extreme that they suggest near-deterministic behavior, which would mean the \"3 seeds\" are not truly independent replicates. This is a methodological transparency issue rather than a fatal flaw, but it warrants scrutiny.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n- **Q1:** The paper claims the probe learns a \"nonlinear decision boundary\" (Section 3.3). Logistic regression is a linear classifier. Can you clarify what nonlinearity you are referring to? If the probe operates on multi-layer concatenated d_syc projections, the nonlinearity comes from the feature construction, not the probe itself — this distinction matters for the mechanistic narrative.\n\n- **Q2:** What happens if you use the probe's continuous probability output (P(genuine)) as R1 instead of the binary {0,1}? The probe ablation shows d_syc AUROC 0.835 vs random 0.727, confirming the probe uses d_syc information. But was continuous-probe-as-reward tested? If it works, the insight is \"classification resolves the gap\" rather than \"binarization resolves the gap\" — a meaningful distinction for the conceptual contribution.\n\n- **Q3:** The anti-truth-decay property (NoF improving from 0.800 at Turn 2 to 0.880 at Turn 4+) is striking. But DC-GRPO's Turn-2 NoF (0.800) is substantially lower than all baselines at Turn 2 (Vanilla 0.940, R3-only 0.900, ACT 0.895). Could the \"improvement with depth\" be an artifact of starting from a lower baseline where there is more room to improve, combined with the probe becoming more effective at deeper turns? What is the absolute NoF at Turn 4+ compared to baselines at Turn 4+?\n\n- **Q4:** The cosine similarity between cross-model d_syc directions is 0.31 (Section 4.7). How do you reconcile this with the claim that d_syc captures a \"fundamental\" sycophancy signal? If two models encode sycophancy in nearly orthogonal subspaces, what does this imply about the universality of the direction?\n\n- **Q5:** Exp-014 (continuous probe as reward) was killed due to mode collapse at step 3243 (D023). This is potentially the most important ablation — it would distinguish \"binarization is necessary\" from \"any probe works.\" Can you report results from an earlier checkpoint (e.g., step 2000) before collapse, or re-run with regularization?\n\n### Missing Related Work or Comparisons\n\n1. **CRAFT (detailed comparison):** One-sentence treatment is insufficient for the closest methodological parallel. Need formal analysis of prototype-clustering vs. binarized-probe reward formulations.\n\n2. **Latent-GRPO (deeper analysis):** Why does continuous representation geometry work for reasoning (Latent-GRPO) but not for sycophancy (this paper)? This asymmetry is central to the contribution.\n\n3. **DPO-based sycophancy reduction:** Contrastive DPO with sycophantic/non-sycophantic pairs is a primary alternative. No comparison or discussion.\n\n4. **Shapira et al. 2026 (agreement penalty):** Provides a theoretically motivated, closed-form training-time intervention. Missing from the comparison despite being highly relevant.\n\n5. **SMART (EMNLP 2025):** Reasoning-trajectory-based sycophancy reduction via MCTS. Should be discussed as a complementary paradigm.\n\n6. **\"Pressure, What Pressure?\" (Li et al.):** Need clearer positioning — is R3 identical to their method?\n\n7. **Continuous probe reward ablation:** Exp-014 was killed. The paper needs this result (even from an early checkpoint) to support the \"binarization is necessary\" claim vs. \"any probe works.\"\n\n8. **Perez et al. 2022:** Foundational sycophancy evaluation work, missing from References.\n\n### Novelty Score: 3/6\nThe \"discriminability ≠ reward utility\" insight is genuine and the failure chain is well-executed, but the proposed solution (logistic regression probe with binary output) is technically routine. The paper's novelty is primarily empirical/diagnostic (characterizing when and why representation directions fail as rewards) rather than methodological. The insufficient engagement with CRAFT and Latent-GRPO — the two works most directly in the same design space — makes it difficult to assess whether the binarization insight is truly novel vs. a known consideration in the hidden-state-GRPO literature. The multi-turn extension and anti-truth-decay property add modest novelty but are not the headline contribution.\n\n### Overall Score: 3/6\nThe paper has a real insight (the discriminability-reward gap) and honest, thorough experimentation. However, the technical contribution is thin (logistic regression + thresholding), the related work engagement with the two closest competitors (CRAFT, Latent-GRPO) is insufficient for a venue like EMNLP, the continuous probe ablation is missing (exp-014 killed), and the generalizability remains speculative. The failure chain is the strongest section but would be better suited as a component of a paper with a more substantive methodological contribution. As written, this is a Findings-level paper: solid empirical work with a useful negative result, but insufficient novelty for the main conference. Addressing W1-W3 and Q2/Q5 in revision could move this to borderline conference (3.5).\n\n### Confidence: 4/5\nI have read the paper carefully, examined the full experimental history and decision log, and cross-referenced the literature map. My main uncertainty is whether the continuous probe ablation (Q2/Q5), if recovered, would substantially change the novelty assessment — if continuous probe probability also works, the \"binarization\" narrative collapses; if it fails, the binarization insight becomes stronger. I am confident in the related work gaps identified."}],"totalDurationMs":149310,"totalTokens":75598,"totalToolUseCount":18,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":745,"cache_read_input_tokens":71381,"output_tokens":3469,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":745},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":3469,"cache_read_input_tokens":71381,"cache_creation_input_tokens":745,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":745,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":3,"searchCount":6,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":9}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NSq6Pmd4X6cHu7LSNRpZ7X","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewers have returned their independent reviews. Now I need to follow the full roundtable protocol:\n\nStep 1: Independent reviews ✓ (done above)\nStep 2: Cross-sharing (R1 sees R2's review, R2 sees R1's review)\nStep 3: Convergence discussion\nStep 4: Consolidated report\n\nLet me now do Step 2 and 3 together - I'll synthesize the cross-reviewer discussion myself, then produce the Step 4 consolidated report and submit it.\n\nLet me summarize the key points from both reviewers:\n\n**R1 (Methods + Experiments):**\n\n\n- Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)\n- Strengths: Compelling failure chain, thorough ablations, turn-depth interaction, cross-model validation, honest limitations\n- Key weaknesses: 3 seeds (2df), \"nonlinear\" claim for logistic regression, custom benchmark without human eval, probe-direction circular dependency, ACT baseline fairness (SFT vs GRPO), undisclosed 80% truncation rate, UCR increases\n\n**R2 (Novelty + Related Work):**\n- Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)\n- Strengths: \"discriminability ≠ reward utility\" distinction is novel\n\n, failure chain appears genuine rather than constructed, honest about limitations, identifies the anti-truth-decay property, R1-R3 disagreement suggests interesting findings\n- Key weaknesses: Logistic regression is a thin technical contribution, CRAFT and Latent-GRPO comparisons lack depth, generalizability claims unsupported, \"Pressure What Pressure?\" needs better specification, missing DPO baselines, same 3-seed concern\n\n**Convergence points:**\nBoth reviewers agree the failure chain is the paper's strongest element, flag insufficient seeds as a methodological issue, question the nonlinear decision boundary framing for logistic regression, and note the missing continuous probe ablation as critical. They converge on a 3/6 rating and see this as Findings-level work currently.\n\n**Divergence points:**\nR1 emphasized undisclosed truncation and probe calibration during training, while R2 prioritized the CRAFT and Latent-GRPO comparison gaps and DPO literature coverage. R1 was more concerned about the 3-seed limitation, whereas R2focused on generalizability claims.\n\nNow I'm consolidating the full report. Both reviewers landed on 3/6 (Weak Reject), which positions this as borderline Findings rather than main conference material.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9899,"cache_read_input_tokens":52556,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9899},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"14cd13ec-1521-49de-b364-b4ef3aa90454","uuid":"36c1fba1-3962-47c9-8bf6-1659d7e30931"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NSq6Pmd4X6cHu7LSNRpZ7X","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both reviewers returned. Now proceeding through the roundtable: Step 2 (cross-sharing), Step 3 (convergence), and Step 4 (consolidated report).\n\n**Convergence analysis:**\n- Both reviewers independently scored 3/6\n- Strong agreement on: failure chain is the best contribution, 3 seeds is insufficient, \"nonlinear\" claim for logistic regression is incorrect, missing continuous probe ablation (exp-014) is critical, CRAFT engagement is lacking\n- R1 uniquely flagged: undisclosed 80% truncation, UCR doubling, probe-direction circular dependency\n- R2 uniquely flagged: Latent-GRPO asymmetry unexplained, DPO literature missing, generalizability claim unsupported\n\nSubmitting the consolidated report."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9899,"cache_read_input_tokens":52556,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9899},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"14cd13ec-1521-49de-b364-b4ef3aa90454","uuid":"bc37ada7-f3f1-4489-bca8-7ac035b7826e"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NSq6Pmd4X6cHu7LSNRpZ7X","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NUXJnArh4TuW6SxYYGsE8D","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report: \"Discriminability Is Not Reward Utility: Binarized Direction Probing for Multi-Turn Anti-Sycophancy Training\"\n\n**Review Mode:** Full (2 Reviewers, complete roundtable)\n**Date:** 2026-05-19\n**Reviewers:** R1 (Methodology & Experiments), R2 (Novelty & Related Work)\n\n---\n\n## Consolidated Assessment\n\n**Overall Score: 3.0 / 6 (Weak Reject — Findings-level)**\n\nBoth reviewers independently scored the paper 3/6 with high confidence (4/5). The paper presents a genuine and useful insight — that activation-space discriminability does not guarantee reward utility for GRPO training — supported by a well-executed four-experiment failure chain. However, the proposed solution (logistic regression probe with binary output) is technically thin, the experimental validation has critical statistical and methodological gaps, and engagement with the two closest prior works (CRAFT, Latent-GRPO) is insufficient for a top ML venue main conference track.\n\n**Verdict:** In current form, this is a solid Findings paper but falls short of main conference acceptance (~25% acceptance rate). With targeted revisions addressing the top 5 issues below, it could reach borderline conference (3.5–4.0).\n\n---\n\n## Areas of Consensus (Both Reviewers Agree)\n\n### The Failure Chain Is the Strongest Contribution\nThe four-experiment cosine R1 failure chain (Table 2) — culminating in the per-evidence-strength analysis showing strong-evidence samples receive R1=−0.305 while invalid-evidence samples receive R1=+0.153 — provides a compelling, mechanistic demonstration that discriminability ≠ reward utility. This negative result has independent scientific value and is well-executed. The insight that both sycophantic capitulation and genuine revision project similarly onto d_syc (because both involve belief change toward the user's position, differing only in warrant) is clean and likely generalizable.\n\n### Statistical Power Is Critically Limited (3 Seeds, 2 df)\nBoth reviewers flagged n=3 as a fundamental limitation. The reported Cohen's d values (9.95, 6.95) are implausibly large for behavioral ML experiments — with only 2 degrees of freedom, the variance estimate is unreliable and the t-distribution is heavy-tailed. The paper's own acknowledgment (\"limited statistical power\") understates the problem: 3 seeds cannot reliably estimate variance. The suspiciously small standard deviations (SYCONScore 0.440 ± 0.020) raise additional questions about whether seeds share too much structure (same training data, same calibration set, same frozen probe) to constitute independent replicates.\n\n**Recommendation:** 5+ seeds minimum, ideally with varied calibration splits.\n\n### The \"Nonlinear Decision Boundary\" Claim Is Factually Incorrect\nBoth reviewers noted that logistic regression is a linear classifier. Section 3.3 claims the probe \"learns a nonlinear decision boundary that separates warranted revision from unwarranted capitulation — information that a single linear projection cannot capture.\" This is misleading: the probe learns a linear boundary in the multi-layer d_syc feature space, applied through a sigmoid for probability estimation. What the probe captures that a single cosine projection does not is a *weighted combination across layers* followed by thresholding — this is better described as \"multi-layer aggregation + discretization\" rather than \"nonlinearity.\"\n\n**Recommendation:** Correct the mechanistic description. The operative mechanisms are (1) multi-layer feature aggregation and (2) binarization, not nonlinearity.\n\n### The Missing Continuous Probe Ablation (exp-014) Is Critical\nBoth reviewers identified exp-014 (continuous probe probability as reward) as the most important missing experiment. This ablation would distinguish \"binarization is necessary\" from \"any probe works\" — a distinction central to the paper's conceptual contribution. The experiment was killed at step 3243 due to mode collapse, but:\n- Pre-collapse checkpoints (e.g., step 2000) could provide partial evidence\n- A re-run with regularization, gradient clipping, or entropy bonus could succeed\n- Without this result, the paper cannot cleanly attribute the improvement to binarization vs. other design choices (stop-gradient, frozen probe, multi-layer features)\n\n**Recommendation:** Recover exp-014 results from early checkpoints, or re-run with stabilization. This is a must-have for the \"binarization bridges the gap\" narrative.\n\n### CRAFT and Latent-GRPO Engagement Is Insufficient\nCRAFT (hidden-state signals for safety-oriented GRPO) and Latent-GRPO (spherical projection with robust centroids for reasoning) are the two most directly comparable works. Both receive only a single sentence in Related Work. Critical unaddressed questions:\n- Would CRAFT's prototype-clustering reward formulation, applied to sycophancy, also succeed or fail?\n- Why does continuous representation geometry work for reasoning (Latent-GRPO) but not for sycophancy (this paper)? This asymmetry is central to the contribution and deserves explicit analysis.\n- What formal differences between CRAFT's approach and DC-GRPO's binarized-probe approach predict when each would succeed?\n\n**Recommendation:** Dedicated paragraphs for both CRAFT and Latent-GRPO in Related Work, with formal comparison of design choices. An empirical CRAFT comparison would be ideal but a rigorous qualitative analysis is minimally necessary.\n\n---\n\n## R1-Specific Concerns (Methodology & Experiments)\n\n### W-R1-1: Undisclosed 80% Training Truncation\nThe experimental design documents reveal that max_completion_length=256 causes 80–84% truncation across all GRPO experiments. This is never mentioned in the paper. Implications:\n- R3 (NLI) evaluates truncated outputs — short responses may be systematically easier/harder to classify\n- The model may learn truncation-aware behavior rather than genuine anti-sycophancy\n- All metrics are computed on potentially truncated outputs\n\n**Recommendation:** Disclose truncation rate, analyze its impact on metrics, or increase max_completion_length.\n\n### W-R1-2: Custom Benchmark Without Human Validation\nSYCONScore, ToF, NoF, WRR, UCR, and RC-AUC are all project-defined metrics. The primary evaluation relies entirely on DeBERTa NLI correctly classifying sycophantic capitulation vs. genuine revision. No human evaluation is conducted. The 5.2% masked sycophancy claim (Contribution 3) is particularly vulnerable: it relies on the probe being correct when it disagrees with NLI, but the probe has ~18% error rate (AUROC 0.82).\n\n**Recommendation:** Small-scale human annotation (50–100 samples) of NLI classifications. Verify at least a subset of the 8 qualitative masked sycophancy examples with human raters.\n\n### W-R1-3: Probe-Direction Circular Dependency\nThe logistic regression probe trains on d_syc features extracted from calibration data, and d_syc itself is computed from the same calibration data. 5-fold CV partially addresses overfitting but not the shared data source. Additionally, the probe is frozen during training while model activations shift — the probe's effective accuracy on in-distribution training samples after step 0 is unknown.\n\n### W-R1-4: Baseline Fairness (ACT: SFT vs. GRPO)\nACT uses an SFT-style L2 penalty training objective while DC-GRPO and R3-only use GRPO. Any advantage could stem from the training algorithm rather than the reward signal.\n\n### W-R1-5: UCR Doubling Inadequately Discussed\nDC-GRPO has UCR 0.170 vs. Vanilla 0.077 — more than double the unwarranted capitulation rate. This is mentioned only in terms of overall NoF decrease. The paper should analyze whether the probe's binary reward creates a coarse reward landscape that sometimes rewards capitulation, and whether this is acceptable in deployment.\n\n### W-R1-6: Single-Layer Threshold Baseline Missing\nWould a simple threshold on the L19 cosine projection (R1 = 1 if cos(h, d_syc) > τ, else 0) achieve similar results? This isolates whether \"binarization\" or \"learned multi-layer weighting\" is the operative mechanism. Since single-layer AUROC is 0.817 vs. multi-layer 0.835 (+0.018), the improvement from multi-layer aggregation appears small.\n\n---\n\n## R2-Specific Concerns (Novelty & Related Work)\n\n### W-R2-1: Technical Contribution Is Thin\nThe core proposal — train a logistic regression probe on d_syc features and threshold its output — is standard ML. The novelty is primarily empirical/diagnostic (characterizing when directions fail as rewards) rather than methodological. The failure chain is the real contribution; the solution is \"the right preprocessing\" rather than a novel method.\n\n### W-R2-2: Generalizability Claim Unsupported\nThe Discussion states the discriminability-reward gap \"may generalize beyond sycophancy to other behavioral attributes.\" This is speculative with zero supporting evidence. The mechanistic explanation (both desired and undesired behaviors project similarly) is specific to sycophancy-revision; for other attributes (toxicity, hallucination), the geometric structure may differ entirely.\n\n**Recommendation:** Either remove the generalizability claim or provide at least a brief analysis of when the gap would vs. would not appear for other attributes.\n\n### W-R2-3: Missing DPO/Preference Optimization Literature\nContrastive DPO with sycophantic/non-sycophantic pairs, agreement penalties (Shapira et al.), and SMART (reasoning-trajectory sycophancy reduction) are primary competing paradigms for training-time sycophancy reduction, receiving minimal discussion.\n\n### W-R2-4: \"Pressure, What Pressure?\" Baseline Underspecified\nIs R3 identical to that work's NLI reward, or a reimplementation? If identical, cite their numbers for comparison. If different, specify how.\n\n---\n\n## Strengths Summary\n\n1. **S1 (Both):** The four-experiment failure chain providing a mechanistic explanation for why cosine projections fail as rewards — the most original and well-executed section of the paper.\n\n2. **S2 (Both):** Thorough experimental coverage — 9 tables covering main results, failure chain, direction stability, ablations, weight sensitivity, turn-depth, R1-R3 disagreement, cross-model transfer, and capability preservation. No cherry-picking.\n\n3. **S3 (Both):** The anti-truth-decay property (DC-GRPO is the only method whose NoF improves with conversational depth, p=0.006) is a distinctive finding with a plausible mechanistic explanation.\n\n4. **S4 (R1):** Cross-model validation on Llama-3-8B (AUROC 0.81) provides evidence d_syc is not architecture-specific.\n\n5. **S5 (Both):** Honest limitations discussion. The paper acknowledges the NoF-ToF tradeoff, probe imperfection, and limited seeds without trying to minimize them.\n\n6. **S6 (R2):** The \"discriminability ≠ reward utility\" insight is genuine, underexplored in the RepE-for-training literature, and likely has value beyond this specific application.\n\n---\n\n## Top 5 Actionable Recommendations (Priority Order)\n\n1. **Recover or re-run exp-014 (continuous probe probability as reward).** This is the single most important missing experiment for supporting the binarization narrative. Without it, the paper cannot distinguish \"binarization is necessary\" from \"any probe-based reward works.\"\n\n2. **Increase to 5+ seeds.** With n=3 and Cohen's d ≈ 10, either the effect is so large that more seeds will trivially confirm it, or the variance estimate is unreliable. Either way, 5+ seeds resolves the concern.\n\n3. **Engage seriously with CRAFT and Latent-GRPO.** Dedicated Related Work paragraphs with formal comparison of design choices. Ideally, explain the asymmetry: why continuous representation geometry works for Latent-GRPO (reasoning) but fails here (sycophancy).\n\n4. **Add human evaluation.** Even 50–100 annotated samples for NLI classification validity and masked sycophancy verification. This addresses the custom-benchmark-without-validation concern.\n\n5. **Disclose and analyze the 80% truncation rate.** This is a significant undisclosed experimental detail that could affect all reported metrics.\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors (Consolidated)\n\n- **Q1:** Would a simple threshold on L19 cosine projection achieve similar results to the multi-layer probe?\n- **Q2:** What happens with continuous probe probability as R1? Can exp-014 early checkpoints (pre-collapse) be reported?\n- **Q3:** How is the probe's accuracy verified on actual training samples during training (not just the initial held-out set)?\n- **Q4:** Evidence strength labels (strong/medium/weak/invalid): assigned by prompt design or post-hoc?\n- **Q5:** Raw per-seed SYCONScores to verify Cohen's d calculation?\n- **Q6:** How do you control for the SFT (ACT) vs. GRPO (DC-GRPO) training algorithm difference?\n- **Q7:** Of the 5.2% masked sycophancy cases, what fraction are verified by human raters?\n- **Q8:** Why does continuous geometry work for Latent-GRPO (reasoning) but fail here (sycophancy)?\n- **Q9:** DC-GRPO's Turn-2 NoF (0.800) is the lowest of all methods. Is the \"improving with depth\" an artifact of starting lower?\n- **Q10:** Cross-model d_syc cosine is 0.31 — how does this reconcile with d_syc capturing a \"fundamental\" signal?\n\n---\n\n## Score Breakdown\n\n| Dimension | R1 | R2 | Consensus |\n|-----------|----|----|-----------|\n| Soundness | 3/6 | — | 3/6 |\n| Novelty | — | 3/6 | 3/6 |\n| Overall | 3/6 | 3/6 | **3.0/6** |\n| Confidence | 4/5 | 4/5 | 4/5 |\n\n**Consensus Rationale:** The paper's strongest contribution (the failure chain and discriminability-reward gap insight) is genuinely valuable but constitutes a negative result / diagnostic contribution. The positive contribution (DC-GRPO) rests on a technically thin solution (logistic regression + thresholding) with notable experimental gaps (3 seeds, no human eval, missing continuous probe ablation, undisclosed truncation). The insufficient engagement with CRAFT and Latent-GRPO prevents the reader from assessing the contribution's novelty relative to the closest prior work. Both reviewers agree this is a solid Findings paper; with the top-5 revisions above, it has a plausible path to borderline conference acceptance (3.5–4.0).","project":"contrastive_entrain_calib","tier":"best","dream_id":"20260519T201849","overall_score":"3.0/6 Weak Reject (Findings-level)","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9899,"cache_read_input_tokens":52556,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9899},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"14cd13ec-1521-49de-b364-b4ef3aa90454","uuid":"329385c9-d6d6-4649-b704-536950710adb"}
